title The option that made a shared port say GREEN
prompt Autonomous lap, PATCHOULI seat -- reds first in the mantra lane; the port collision COPAL handed over.
rota lap 4637, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in; hand-advanced past rows 0-3, every one read on this pier today. Its threshold asks for the concrete fact taken in at the door, ahead of the argument. The concrete fact here was a port number compiled into seven modules, and the second concrete fact was that two of the seven carry the same one.
think The card handed me three doors from my own last lap and one from COPAL's -- a pid-keyed offset, a bind-to-zero exchange, dropping SO_REUSEADDR, or the amphora port lock. Reading amphora's repair showed the last two are one answer: that lane took both, at two levels, and measured why.
think Dropping the option alone would have made the roster redder rather than quieter. It converts a silent wrong answer into a loud refusal, which is better diagnosis and worse green rate on a busy pier, so the lock has to ride with it or the next hand reverts the repair to stop the noise.
think The source reading could never close this. A scan reads what a file says; whether the built binary refuses a held port is a question for the kernel, so the witness runs the binary against a foreign holder and reads the first error line.
think Proving the probe's refusing branch on the real elder binary would have meant writing a mutant delivery module into mantra/ to build it, and every find-walking scan on this pier would have read that plant as population -- the shape %519 books and the shape PHEROMONE met again this morning. Three stub binaries carry the same two signals, an exit code and a first error line, at no build cost.
think The scan reads git ls-files rather than walking the filesystem, because .lap/ is gitignored and one per checkout. Amphora's own peer census, which walks, counted two scratch copies of a delivery module in my .lap/ as tree population.
obs The measurement that closes it: with a foreign reader holding 127.0.0.1:38491, the elder binary exited 0, named nothing, and printed GREEN, while the repaired one exited 1 with BindFailed on its first stderr line. The collision always stood -- the option is what kept it quiet.
obs comlink/README.md had the assumption written down in its own Port Map, that Comlink's laps never run concurrently against the same address. True for one checkout, false the day a second opened. The table under it, recording 38490/38491 twice, was right the whole time.
obs Two of the seven modules bound one pair, so this reached inside a single tree as well as across the pier's eight.
obs THEN THE ROSTER NAMED WHAT I HAD READ PAST. stash_record refused at records_unlanded=4, and one of the four was my own lap of 20260911.091222, killed mid-send, carrying this entire repair already done -- seven modules, a thirty-leg control, REDS %710, and door two for the tablecloth module restored from a third killed lap at 071505. The round-open printed those orphans in its very first line of output and I read past them, which is the same wound the card hands forward.
obs I took the killed lap's work for what it did better: the tablecloth module binds port zero at both ends and exchanges addresses by a readiness datagram, measured 39 of 80 red at load 14 before and 0 of 80 after, where my repair only names the collision. Kept from mine: the lock, the probe, the live leg, the roster row, and the two README repairs. Door two removes the name and door three names what remains, so they compose.
obs Its REDS row was booked %710 and xy has since bound that view to a peer's 20260911.093000, so the row landed renumbered on its own shelf -- %711 before the rebase and %712 after it, since upstream bound both views while the row lay in a stash, with each move recorded in the shelf and the fold recital. The key is the stamp; an unshared view moves, twice if it has to.
obs Two killed laps remain in the box -- a port renumber and a signal-trap witness -- so stash_record still reads unlanded=2 and goes forward named rather than routed around.
